WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.760
John Lennon as Gollum? A steamy romance&nbsp;
between Frodo and Galadriel? It all could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.160
have happened — but you'll never get to see it.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.880
J.R.R. Tolkien's vast and beloved&nbsp;
legendarium has been portrayed on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.360
screen many times over the years. Long before&nbsp;
the first successful attempt came to fruition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.840
however, many others had tried their hands&nbsp;
at adapting Middle-earth for the movies.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.240
But what did Tolkien himself think&nbsp;
about all this? Well, In 1968,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.920
The Telegraph published an interview with&nbsp;
the author, in which he expanded on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.760
views regarding the rights to adapt his&nbsp;
world for the screen. As it turned out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.880
Tolkien had been solicited countless times by&nbsp;
people hoping to put the Middle-earth stamp on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.760
everything from movies and TV shows to puzzles&nbsp;
and soaps. But his fans at the time weren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:49.120
exactly supportive of these ideas — the article&nbsp;
cites one 17-year-old fan, who wrote to Tolkien:

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.760
"Don't let them make a movie&nbsp;
out of your Ring. It would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.480
like putting Disneyland into the Grand Canyon."

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.560
For his part, it seems that Tolkien agreed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.520
albeit for slightly different&nbsp;
reasons. He told The Telegraph:

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.640
"You can't cramp narrative into dramatic form. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.267
would be easier to film The Odyssey. Much&nbsp;
less happens in it. Only a few storms."

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:10.820
"To all who come to this happy place, welcome!"

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.680
Nowadays, it seems like nearly every major&nbsp;
movie and television franchise in existence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.160
falls under the jurisdiction of the House of&nbsp;
Mouse. Everything from the Marvel Cinematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.720
Universe to the Star Wars galaxy to the Muppets&nbsp;
is owned and controlled by Disney — and there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.360
was a very brief period in which the company&nbsp;
considered staking a claim in Middle-earth.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.560
In 1938, just a year after The Hobbit was&nbsp;
released, a Disney animator suggested combining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.360
elements of the novel with Richard Wagner's&nbsp;
"Ring" cycle of musical dramas for a segment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:45.280
in the animated movie Fantasia. Disney also tried&nbsp;
and failed to produce a film of The Hobbit during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:50.960
the 1950s. Disney animator Wolfgang Reitherman&nbsp;
once claimed that Disney even considered adapting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.360
The Lord of the Rings, but decided it was&nbsp;
too "long and unwieldy" for the big screen.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.240
This may have been for the best, however, because&nbsp;
Tolkien truly detested Disney. In one of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.440
letters, written in 1937, the author claimed&nbsp;
he "loathed" the studio and insisted that any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.640
illustrations for an American publication of The&nbsp;
Hobbit not be influenced by Disney's animations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.280
Tolkien echoed these sentiments in a later&nbsp;
letter, written in 1968, in which he wrote:

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.080
"I recognize [Disney's] talent, but it has&nbsp;
always seemed to me hopelessly corrupted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.520
Though in most of the 'pictures'&nbsp;
proceeding from his studios there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.855
admirable or charming passages, the effect&nbsp;
of all of them to me is disgusting."

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.560
Tolkien's first positive response&nbsp;
to an attempted Hollywood adaptation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.320
of The Lord of the Rings came in&nbsp;
1957. That year, American sci-fi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.520
editor Forrest J. Ackerman and producer Al&nbsp;
Brodax teamed up with writer Morton Grady&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.600
Zimmerman to approach the author with their&nbsp;
idea for an animated Middle-earth movie.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.440
Tolkien was initially impressed by their&nbsp;
illustrations, which he noted to be in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:53.200
the style of celebrated literary&nbsp;
illustrator Arthur Rackham. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.680
less impressed by the proposed storyline,&nbsp;
however, which featured what he called a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.320
"general degradation" of the story. Nevertheless,&nbsp;
the rights were optioned to Team Ackerman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:07.120
partly because Tolkien believed he could acquire&nbsp;
an author's veto on anything he didn't like.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.280
Soon after, Zimmerman sent Tolkien a detailed&nbsp;
treatment of his Lord of the Rings story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.440
Tolkien's response, written to Ackerman in a&nbsp;
letter, was anything but approving. Mostly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.080
he complained about Zimmerman's&nbsp;
rampant carelessness with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.600
narrative. But Tolkien's greatest&nbsp;
criticism had to do with the tone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.960
which he believed was too action-heavy and failed&nbsp;
to accurately represent the "heart of the tale."

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.080
In the end, the Ackerman project&nbsp;
died a pitiful death: the project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:34.119
was shut down after failing to fund his&nbsp;
request for an extension to the rights.

00:03:34.316 --> 00:03:38.800
The whole world was gripped by Beatlemania&nbsp;
in the 1960s. Perhaps inevitably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:43.600
the runaway success of the Fab Four's music was&nbsp;
accompanied by a number of live-action films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.720
including A Hard Day's Night&nbsp;
and Help! It was during this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.640
period that The Beatles attempted to get&nbsp;
a Lord of the Rings movie off the ground.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.400
John Lennon in particular was a fan of&nbsp;
Tolkien, and believed that he and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.800
bandmates could very well star in the picture:&nbsp;
Lennon as Gollum, Paul McCartney as Frodo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:04.320
George Harrison as Gandalf, and Ringo Starr&nbsp;
as Sam. They even approached Stanley Kubrick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:08.560
who had just directed 1964's Dr.&nbsp;
Strangelove, to helm the project.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.800
"Gentlemen, you can't fight in&nbsp;
here — this is the war room!"

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.560
Sadly, it wasn't to be. For one thing, Kubrick&nbsp;
wasn't interested. But it appears that Tolkien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:21.680
himself drove the final nail into The Beatles'&nbsp;
coffin. In 2021, Peter Jackson told the BBC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:26.480
that the author himself had refused the Fab Four&nbsp;
permission to make their movie. Jackson claimed:

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:29.200
"He didn't like the idea of&nbsp;
a pop group doing his story."

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:30.240
The director added:

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:33.760
"What would The Beatles have done with a Lord&nbsp;
of the Rings soundtrack album? That would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:38.880
have been 14 or 15 Beatles songs that would&nbsp;
have been pretty incredible to listen to."

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.040
In 1969, United Artists acquired the&nbsp;
film rights to The Lord of the Rings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:47.440
Tolkien was 77 years old by that point, and&nbsp;
had decided that he would not live to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:52.000
his novel appear on screen. As such, he didn't&nbsp;
particularly care who ended up with the rights.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.320
A few years later, United Artists hired&nbsp;
director John Boorman to helm a live-action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:00.880
adaptation of the novel. Boorman would later&nbsp;
become renowned for movies such as Deliverance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:05.040
and Hope and Glory, but at that time he&nbsp;
had directed just a handful of features.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.440
Screenwriter Rospo Pallenberg worked with&nbsp;
Boorman to figure out how best to bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:14.480
Middle-earth to life on the silver screen. He&nbsp;
ended up writing a 176-page script for their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:18.160
movie. The details of the screenplay&nbsp;
range from fascinating to mortifying:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:22.080
it featured a Kabuki-style theatrical&nbsp;
performance of Middle-earth's history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:28.240
a love scene for Frodo and Galadriel, and a battle&nbsp;
of words fought between Gandalf and Saruman.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:33.120
Boorman's film was destined to fail, however.&nbsp;
United Artists ran out of money after a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.440
of commercial setbacks, finally deciding not to&nbsp;
move forward with the project. The good news,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.720
though, is that the script still&nbsp;
exists online — just in case you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:44.720
want to better imagine a movie in which&nbsp;
Aragorn and Boromir passionately kiss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:49.914
beekeepers defend the walls of Minas Tirith,&nbsp;
and Merry blinds a horse with a frying pan.

00:05:49.914 --> 00:05:53.440
The title of Ralph Bakshi's&nbsp;
animated Middle-earth movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:55.600
The Lord of the Rings, is something of a misnomer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:00.560
as it only tells the story of The Fellowship of&nbsp;
the Ring and certain aspects of The Two Towers.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:06.840
"Gandalf! Beyond all hope, Gandalf!"

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:12.240
Upon its release in 1978, The Lord of the&nbsp;
Rings was a considerable financial success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.080
earning $30 million worldwide&nbsp;
off an estimated $4 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.000
budget. But it was met with poor reviews from&nbsp;
fans, who were not pleased at all with what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.640
they considered to be subpar visuals and the fact&nbsp;
that the movie ended halfway through the story.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:28.880
Part 2 of Bakshi's tale was greenlit&nbsp;
for release in 1980. Initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:32.160
the film's creators were optimistic,&nbsp;
but a sequel to The Lord of the Rings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.240
simply never materialized. Screenwriter&nbsp;
Peter S. Beagle later recalled that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.720
all came down to creative differences&nbsp;
between Bakshi and producer Saul Zaentz.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:45.600
Bakshi in particular believed that the first movie&nbsp;
simply hadn't been as good as it could have been.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:51.280
In 1980, the production company Rankin/Bass,&nbsp;
which had made 1977's animated Hobbit movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:55.680
released The Return of the King as an ABC&nbsp;
television special. While this had nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.000
to do with Bakshi's half-completed project, the&nbsp;
story does pick up around the same point that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:06.080
The Lord of the Rings ended, providing fans with&nbsp;
a kind of unofficial conclusion to the 1978 movie.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:11.440
In the early 1990s. Russian director Roman&nbsp;
Mitrofanov set out to bring an animated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:16.880
adaptation of The Hobbit to life, but the project&nbsp;
fell apart when the Soviet Union crumbled in 1991.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:20.720
Here's the fun part, though: the movie's&nbsp;
six-minute prologue, depicting the fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:25.680
of Erebor and Dale, still exists online.&nbsp;
The sequence opens on a party in Dale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:30.720
in which the townsfolk cavort and dance in&nbsp;
the square. Enter Smaug, who destroys Dale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:35.840
and takes Erebor for his own. As the snow falls,&nbsp;
the scene cuts to a meeting of the dwarves and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:40.320
Gandalf, who suggests they hire a burglar to&nbsp;
accompany them on their quest for vengeance.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.840
You'll probably know all this, of course,&nbsp;
if you've already seen or read The Hobbit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:48.240
But the Mitrofanov prologue features a couple&nbsp;
of interesting quirks, not seen in the novel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.080
that Peter Jackson appears to have&nbsp;
referenced in his movies. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:56.640
the people of Dale fly dragon kites, just&nbsp;
like they do in An Unexpected Journey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.480
And while the animated Erebor looks&nbsp;
much different to Jackson's version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.880
the sight of Dale covered by snow will look&nbsp;
all too familiar to fans of the latter.

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:10.085
"Now it is a ruin… the Desolation of Smaug."

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:12.400
You can only imagine how the Hobbit movies might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:15.586
have looked if Mitrofanov&nbsp;
had finished his adaptation.

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:20.640
A 2021 essay written for Polygon by film&nbsp;
critic Drew McWeeny tells the story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:24.560
the war between Jackson and disgraced&nbsp;
producer Harvey Weinstein over the Lord&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:28.320
of the Rings movies. Having worked with&nbsp;
Jackson on many of his previous films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:32.800
Weinstein acquired the rights to The Lord of the&nbsp;
Rings from Saul Zaentz and hired the director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:37.360
and his screenwriting partners, Fran Walsh&nbsp;
and Philippa Boyens, to make it a reality.

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:42.000
The first outline Jackson and Walsh prepared&nbsp;
for Miramax, Weinstein's company, told a story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:46.560
across two pictures: The Fellowship of the Ring&nbsp;
and The War of the Ring. McWeeny writes that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:51.920
original scripts felt like watching the final&nbsp;
movies at 1.5 times the speed — many characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:56.720
get little development, the Shire scenes are&nbsp;
short, and the elves are met only briefly.

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:59.600
Weinstein wasn't exactly&nbsp;
cooperative during pre-production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:04.480
and eventually restricted Jackson to a&nbsp;
measly $75 million budget. So Jackson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:08.000
and Walsh decided on a desperate gamble&nbsp;
to save their movie: they leaked the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.640
script for their project to McWeeny, who&nbsp;
then reviewed it on Ain't It Cool News.

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:16.800
Weinstein pushed Jackson even further after&nbsp;
that, demanding The Lord of the Rings become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:22.080
one four-hour film. Jackson refused and Weinstein&nbsp;
conceded, giving up the movie on the condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:27.040
that Miramax be repaid everything that had&nbsp;
already been spent on development. Luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:31.040
New Line execs Bob Shaye and Michael De&nbsp;
Luca rode in to save the day, committing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:36.664
to the project and insisting on three Lord of the&nbsp;
Rings movies. The rest, as they say, is history.

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:42.048
"In a hole in the ground, there lived a hobbit."

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:47.520
In 2008, J.R.R. Tolkien and Guillermo del&nbsp;
Toro fans alike were overjoyed to learn that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:52.480
the acclaimed fantasy/horror director had been&nbsp;
hired to helm New Line Cinema's Hobbit movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:56.960
They were somewhat less overjoyed to learn, two&nbsp;
years later, that del Toro had left the project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:00.960
citing production delays and other commitments&nbsp;
as the reasons for his departure. For better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:04.720
or for worse, the Hobbit trilogy would&nbsp;
eventually be directed by Peter Jackson.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:09.200
So how would Del Toro's Middle-earth saga have&nbsp;
played out? Well, for one thing, it was intended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:14.320
to take place over the course of two movies rather&nbsp;
than three: An Unexpected Journey and There and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:20.080
Back Again. In 2011, New Yorker writer Daniel&nbsp;
Zalewski visited del Toro's home and got a glimpse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:25.120
at how the films may have looked. He described&nbsp;
seeing a sketch of Smaug the dragon that resembled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:30.160
a "flying axe" and drawings of other new monsters,&nbsp;
such as an armor-plated troll that could curl up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:35.280
into a ball. Del Toro also elaborated on making&nbsp;
Middle-earth look like a psychedelic painting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:40.160
rather than the realistic New Zealand landscapes&nbsp;
that Peter Jackson eventually utilized.

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:43.520
Of course, it's impossible to say whether&nbsp;
Del Toro's Hobbit movies would have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:45.200
any better or worse than Jackson's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:49.149
but one thing is for sure: they clearly&nbsp;
would have made for one heck of a trip.

